blocks.md:
But who awaits the outermost future? `main` cannot be `async` on its own. Rust deliberately ships **no async runtime** in the standard library, so different programs (a web server, an embedded device) can choose one that fits. A **runtime** provides an **executor** that polls futures, plus async versions of I/O, timers and channels. The most widely used is **Tokio**; *The Rust Programming Language* wraps one in its teaching crate `trpl`:

```rust
fn main() {
    let args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();
    trpl::block_on(async {
        let url = &args[1];
        match page_title(url).await {
            Some(title) => println!("The title for {url} was {title}"),
            None => println!("{url} had no title"),
        }
    })
}
```

With Tokio you would write `#[tokio::main] async fn main()`, a macro that expands to the same `block_on` call.

